Closes #142718

## What Problem This Solves

Fixes TypeScript errors when a plugin gateway handler returns a response payload.

## User Impact

Plugin authors can return payloads synchronously or asynchronously without a cast, while existing `respond(...)` handlers keep working.

## Why This Change Was Made

The plugin-facing signature now reflects the adapter’s existing return-value completion behavior. Core callback handler types, explicit response precedence, and dispatch authority stay unchanged. The adapter’s emitted JavaScript is byte-identical after TypeScript erasure.

## Evidence

- Conflict refresh: rebased the existing change onto main `abf88cbc0a4484faeab8f80db4ce35200f6ff53e`; current head is `fe0483d8b625cb93d35211b3dac86ceade957907`. The runtime adapter retains current main’s capability-removal error handling. The previous refreshed head passed test types but failed formatting and the repository test-lines ratchet (the repository total exceeded its ceiling, with this change adding 21 test lines). This head adjusts formatting; CI is pending; the compiler, Gateway and hosted results below describe the earlier head.

- A cast-free external plugin importing `openclaw/plugin-sdk/plugin-entry` produces six expected compiler errors against clean-base built declarations and compiles against the candidate. Both synchronous and asynchronous callback handlers compile on the base. The candidate also compiles through `openclaw/plugin-sdk/core`.
- The exact same TypeScript plugin was loaded through normal plugin startup in an isolated real Gateway. All eight WebSocket cases passed: async/sync payloads, `false`, `null`, sync/async callbacks, explicit success precedence, and explicit error precedence. The fixture used only `operator.read`; no admission or authorization bypass was used.
- Nine focused tests passed across the existing SDK and loader suites. The changed SDK suite took 21.64 seconds locally, with 48 ms across six test bodies; no additional Gateway/process boot was added to the test suite. Per-test CI timing is not isolated here.
- The canonical `core-test-plugin-sdk` type graph passed, including the public entrypoint equivalence and unchanged `void | Promise<void>` core handler assertions.
- Canonical SDK declaration generation passed for the `core` and `plugin-entry` roots with normal compiler/boundary hooks. The serial canonical `qaRuntime` build and its postbuild checks passed.

Hosted validation: [CI run 37934837105](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/actions/runs/37934837105) and its required `openclaw/ci-gate` passed on head `aae51ba92cd0749527fb9a0ae522bf7d968de758` (36 successful jobs, 27 planned skips). Compiler logs confirm 36 TypeScript configurations, including core/UI/extensions production, all four root-test partitions, and the SDK owning graph, on the verified merge of this head with main. CodeQL and Security Review passed. [Hosted review](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/pull/167811#issuecomment-6081497261) found sufficient live proof, no findings, and no before-merge items.

Local validation limitations: full core and extensions typechecks ended at exit 137 without TypeScript diagnostics. The root-test run stopped at its first tooling partition with the same exit code. The full SDK declaration aggregate reported `oneshot canceled`; the two selected public roots passed. These local aggregate attempts are not claimed as passing; the hosted type results are recorded above. A full SDK declaration aggregate build is still not claimed.

Thirty-three other changed-check gates passed, including SDK exports/surface, changed-file lint, graph boundaries, import cycles, and database/webhook/pairing guards. The production and full-tree export scans each found the same pre-existing unused QA WhatsApp export, already fixed on main by [04aa1dc](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/commit/04aa1dc395116e07743fd9ce742bceea22bc042d). The frozen candidate does not include that unrelated repair. The script export scan passed. Final independent review: scoped-clean, no P0–P2 findings, with the live proof and validation limitations included.

