Operators running several agents cannot tell which agent's tool calls are failing, slow, or blocked: the tool-execution metrics are fleet-wide, while token, cost and skill metrics already carry `agent`.

## User Impact

User impact: `openclaw_tool_execution_total`, `openclaw_tool_execution_duration_seconds` and `openclaw_tool_execution_blocked_total` gain an `agent` label, so per-agent tool error rate, latency and policy blocks can be graphed next to the existing per-agent token and cost panels.

- **Query shape:** aggregated queries (`sum by (tool) (...)`) are unaffected. A query that reads these series without aggregating now gets one series per agent; `sum without (agent) (...)` restores the previous shape.
- **Cardinality:** the per-family reserve (128 label sets) is unchanged, as for the other agent-labelled families. A deployment with many agents × tools reaches it sooner; overflow stays visible in `openclaw_prometheus_family_series_dropped_total{family="..."}` plus the one-time warning. I left the reserves alone because the shared pool is tight; happy to adjust if you prefer.

## Why This Change Was Made

`tool.execution.*` events already carry an optional `agentId`, and the emitters fill it in when the agent is known (tool wrapper, CLI runner, ACP, Codex app-server). The exporter just didn't map it. This maps it through the same `normalizeDiagnosticValue` policy as `openclaw_skill_used_total`: a missing id renders as `agent="unknown"`, and a session-key-shaped id is replaced by `unknown`, never exported. **Exporter-only**: no event, SDK or core change. The docs label table is updated.

Out of scope: the model-call and run event types also carry `agentId` now (#156018). The same mapping for those families can follow as a separate PR if wanted.

## Evidence

**Real Gateway proof, before/after.** A Gateway started with `openclaw gateway run` (loopback, token auth), with `main` and `ops` configured. Six real agent turns go through the Gateway with `openclaw agent --agent <id>`. A local scripted OpenAI Responses endpoint makes the model call `read`, and a local plugin's `before_tool_call` hook blocks `read` on `blocked.txt`. Then `GET /api/diagnostics/prometheus` with the bearer token (401 without it).

- **Before (`main` `40438e059d3`):** everything merges into fleet-wide series: `outcome="completed"` 3, `outcome="error"` 1, `openclaw_tool_execution_blocked_total` 2, with no way to tell `main` from `ops`.
- **After (this head):** each agent gets its own outcomes, durations and policy blocks:
  - `main`: completed 2 (`duration_seconds_sum` 0.037), blocked 1.
  - `ops`: completed 1 (0.004 s), error 1 (0.003 s), blocked 1.
  - No session key appears in the scrape.
- **Not covered:** a live provider. The model is a local fixture, so tool calls are deterministic. Gateway, agent runtime, tool wrapper, plugin hook, exporter and HTTP auth are all the real code paths.

**Tests.** `pnpm test:extension diagnostics-prometheus`: 60/60 pass. With the two-line mapping reverted, 4 fail: the new test (`splits tool execution metrics by agent without exporting session-shaped agent ids`) and the three existing tool-metric assertions updated here.

**Cost** (`pnpm test <file> --maxWorkers=1`, local, cold transform cache):
- `service.test.ts`: 201 s wall, 98% transform; tests ≈ 0.36 s, new test 8 ms.
- `service.subscription.test.ts`: 35 s wall; tests ≈ 0.85 s.
- CI seconds: see this PR's checks.

**Static checks.** Type-aware `oxlint` (`--tsconfig extensions/tsconfig.json`), `oxfmt --check`, `pnpm tsgo:extensions`, `pnpm tsgo:extensions:test`, `git diff --check`: clean.

