## What Problem This Solves

Fixes: when Twilio refuses an outbound call, the agent can't tell why. The `voice_call` result carries only Twilio's message, and Twilio uses the same message for several different refusals.

## User Impact

User impact: a failed Twilio call now reports Twilio's error code and docs link, e.g. `Twilio API error: 400 Account not allowed to call +212XXXXXXXXX (Twilio error 21216: https://www.twilio.com/docs/errors/21216)`. The agent, or the person it is talking to, can now tell a trial-account limit, a geo-permission block and a blocked destination apart, and open the right fix page.

## Why This Change Was Made

`TwilioApiError` already parsed `code` for internal use (the 21220 redirect race). It dropped `code` and `more_info` from the message, and the message is the only part that reaches the caller: `manager/outbound.ts` returns `{ success: false, error: formatErrorMessage(err) }`. This change appends both to the message when the response has them and exposes `moreInfo` beside `twilioCode`. Only `https://` links are kept. Bodies that aren't JSON, or have no code, keep their current message.

## Evidence

- A real outbound call on a Full Twilio account to a Moroccan number with geo permissions enabled was refused with HTTP 400, body `{"code":21216,"message":"Account not allowed to call +212XXXXXXXXX","more_info":"https://www.twilio.com/docs/errors/21216",...}` (number redacted). The tool result showed only `Twilio API error: 400 Account not allowed to call +212XXXXXXXXX`, which reads like a geo-permission problem. Enabling the country, and later its high-risk prefixes, changed nothing. The code pointed at the real cause.
- Live, through the tool's path: the `initiate_call` command (`createVoiceCallCommandService().initiate` → `CallManager.initiateCall` → `TwilioProvider`) against the real Twilio API, with a made-up account SID (no real credentials; script below). The JSON is what `voice_call` returns (`index.ts`: `json({ error: formatErrorMessage(err) })`):

  ```text
  base 2937d658
  {"error":"Twilio API error: 401 auth account AC00000000000000000000000000000000 does not exist"}
  fix e2e05f67
  {"error":"Twilio API error: 401 auth account AC00000000000000000000000000000000 does not exist (Twilio error 20003: https://www.twilio.com/docs/errors/20003)"}
  ```

